<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanak people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kanaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kanak are the indigenous Melanesian people of New Caledonia, making up about 40 per cent of its population. French colonisation from 1853 confined them to reserves and provoked major revolts in 1878 and 1917. The independence movement, led from 1984 by the FLNKS, brought violent conflict in the 1980s that ended with the Matignon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noumea">Nouméa</a> accords, granting wider autonomy and referendums on independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanak-people.jpg" alt="Kanak people" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WarX · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_FLNKS.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kanak are the indigenous Melanesian inhabitants of New Caledonia in the south-western Pacific. They traditionally speak diverse Austronesian languages of the New Caledonian branch of Oceanic. Melanesian settlement on Grande Terre dates back at least to the Lapita culture, about 3000 BP, and obsidian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> was found with the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapita-pottery">Lapita pottery</a> there. Their origin is unclear.</p><p>The word Kanak derives from the Hawaiian kanaka maoli, 'ordinary person', which Europeans applied to Pacific islanders. Before European contact there were no unified states in New Caledonia and no single name for its people. In 1984 Melanesian leaders adopted the spelling Kanak in place of the colonial Canaque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> landed in 1774, when there were reportedly 70,000 Kanaks, and named the islands New Caledonia. French Catholics arrived in 1843, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the islands in 1853. Kanaks were used as forced labour on French plantations, ranches and public works, and moved to reserves covering only 10 per cent of their ancestral lands, their numbers having fallen sharply through disease. France sent 20,000 convicts between 1864 and 1897 and mined nickel from 1864 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from 1875. The Kanak revolt of 1878 was suppressed by the better-armed French; the Kanak leader was decapitated and his head displayed in the Museum of Natural History.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> listed New Caledonia as a non-self-governing territory in 1946, and Kanaks gained voting rights in 1951. The FLNKS was formed in 1984. The Ouvéa cave hostage taking ended with 21 deaths, 19 of them Kanaks. The Matignon Accord of 6 November 1988 proposed a referendum on independence; Jean-Marie Tjibaou, who signed it, was assassinated on 4 May 1989 by a Kanak activist. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noumea-accord">Nouméa Accord</a> of 5 May 1998 gave a degree of autonomy. Referendums in 2018, 2020 and 2021 rejected independence, the last boycotted by pro-independence Kanaks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>According to the 2019 census Kanaks make up 41.2 per cent of New Caledonia's population, about 112,000 people. The Jean-Marie Tjibaou Cultural Centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noumea">Nouméa</a>, formally established in May 1998, followed Tjibaou's proposal for an agency to develop Kanak culture. Wooden carvings of hawks, ancient gods, serpents and turtles are popular. Music, dance and song form part of ceremonies that cement relations within the clan and with ancestors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanak people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The indigenous <b>Kanak</b> were confined to reserves under the indigénat code, and they rose in major revolts in 1878, led by Ataï, and in 1917.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-caledonia">New Caledonia</a></li></ul></section>
